## Summary
This PR improves developer experience by adding a basic, non-breaking classification
for network errors in the browser XHR adapter.

## Problem
Currently, different network failure scenarios in browsers (e.g., no internet connection,
DNS issues, CORS failures) are all reported as a generic `"Network Error"`.

Due to browser security restrictions, detailed error information is not available,
making debugging difficult.

## Solution
This change introduces a simple heuristic:

- Uses `navigator.onLine` to detect offline state
- Adds a new property `error.isOffline = true` when the user is offline
- Keeps existing error message and error code unchanged

## Notes
- This is a best-effort improvement due to browser limitations
- Does not introduce new error codes to maintain backward compatibility

## Example

```js
try {
  await axios.get('/api');
} catch (error) {
  if (error.isOffline) {
    console.log('User is offline');
  } else {
    console.log(error.message);
  }
}
```

Refs: #10735

The xhr adapter's `onabort`, `onerror`, `ontimeout`, and `onCanceled` handlers reject the promise but never run `done()`, so the listener they added to `cancelToken` or `AbortSignal` stays attached. If you reuse one token or controller for many requests, this turns into a memory leak: every failed request retains a listener holding the full config.

Quick repro:

```js
const { XMLHttpRequest } = require('xmlhttprequest');
globalThis.XMLHttpRequest = XMLHttpRequest;
const axios = require('axios');

(async () => {
  const source = axios.CancelToken.source();
  for (let i = 0; i < 500; i++) {
    await axios.get('http://127.0.0.1:1/', {
      cancelToken: source.token,
      adapter: 'xhr',
      timeout: 500,
      data: { blob: new Uint8Array(100_000).fill(i & 0xff) },
    }).catch(() => {});
  }

  console.log('leaked listeners:', source.token._listeners.length);
  // v1.15.1: 500 (about 550 MB retained after GC)
  // with this fix: 0
})();
```

The fix just adds a `done()` call to each of the four error handlers. The success path and the http/fetch adapters already do this.

