## Summary

Since v1.15.2, the http adapter installs a **once-per-socket** `'error'` listener (`handleSocketError`, the fix for #10780's listener accumulation). That listener is an **inline closure created inside `dispatchHttpRequest`** and is never removed — so via its scope chain it retains the **entire adapter scope of whichever request first touched the socket** for the socket's whole lifetime: the settled response promise (`reactions_or_result` → the full response body), the buffered body chunks, `boundSockets`, timers.

With keep-alive agent pools that stay warm (long-running services), every pooled socket permanently pins one complete response (~body size × 2: string on-heap + chunk buffers off-heap). On our scraper fleet (agentkeepalive pools up to 600 sockets, ~3 MB HTML responses) this retained **multiple GB per process** and OOM-killed pods continuously. The leak grows with socket churn and is invisible to handle counts — heap and external memory grow ~1:1 while active handles stay flat, and a full GC reclaims nothing.

Affects v1.15.2 through current v1.x head (verified in 1.16.0, 1.17.0, 1.18.1 and this branch).

## Evidence (heap snapshot from production, axios 1.17.0)

Retainer path for a 3.2 MB retained response string:

```
TCP handle (GC root, live pooled socket)
 → Socket → _events.error[…] = closure handleSocketError
 → closure context chain (adapter scope of the FIRST request on this socket)
 → context:reject → _reject → Promise → reactions_or_result
 → response object → .data → <3.2 MB response body string>
```

A snapshot of one process showed 1046 Socket/TLSSocket objects in heap vs ~295 live socket handles, 766 retained `IncomingMessage`s, and hundreds of MB of response strings + `JSArrayBufferData`, all rooted in `handleSocketError` closures on pooled or dead sockets.

## Repro

<details>
<summary>Standalone script (node --expose-gc repro.js) — measures post-GC memory while the pool is warm</summary>

```js
const http = require('http');
const axios = require('axios');
const Agent = require('agentkeepalive'); // plain http.Agent({keepAlive:true}) works too

const BIG = '<!doctype html>' + 'x'.repeat(3 * 1024 * 1024);
const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  if (!req.url.startsWith('/final')) { res.writeHead(302, {location: `/final${req.url}`}); res.end(); return; }
  res.writeHead(200, {'content-type': 'text/html'}); res.end(BIG);
});

server.listen(0, () => {
  const port = server.address().port;
  const agent = new Agent({ keepAlive: true, maxSockets: 30, maxFreeSockets: 30, freeSocketTimeout: 4000 });
  const inst = axios.create({ timeout: 600, maxRedirects: 1 });
  const url = () => `http://127.0.0.1:${port}/p${Math.floor(Math.random() * 1000)}`;
  const t0 = Date.now();
  let inflight = 0;
  const pump = () => {
    while (inflight < 30 && Date.now() - t0 < 45000) {
      inflight++;
      inst.get(url(), { httpAgent: agent }).catch(() => {}).finally(() => { inflight--; setImmediate(pump); });
    }
  };
  pump();
  setInterval(() => {
    global.gc(); global.gc();
    const m = process.memoryUsage();
    console.log(`heapUsed=${(m.heapUsed / 1048576) | 0}Mi external=${(m.external / 1048576) | 0}Mi`);
    if (Date.now() - t0 > 47000) process.exit(0);
  }, 5000);
});
```

</details>

Results (30-socket warm pool, 3 MB bodies, post-GC):

| | heapUsed | external |
|---|---|---|
| v1.17.0 stock | 73–116 Mi retained | 70–111 Mi retained |
| this fix | 12–16 Mi flat | 4–17 Mi flat |

The retained memory frees only when the sockets die — confirming the retainer is the per-socket listener, not the requests.

## Fix

Hoist the listener to a module-scope function that reads the active request via `this[kAxiosCurrentReq]` (`EventEmitter` invokes listeners with `this` bound to the emitter, i.e. the socket). It captures no per-request context; the once-per-socket install guard, the `kAxiosCurrentReq` swap, and the error semantics (destroy the currently-active request) are unchanged.

